WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:03.388
I was now in my fourth year of apprenticeship to Joe. It was a Saturday night, and

2
00:00:03.389 --> 00:00:11.213
we were at the pub. A group of us sat around the fire listening to Mr. Wopsle

3
00:00:11.214 --> 00:00:14.618
read the newspaper aloud. I soon became aware of a strange gentleman looking on. When Mr. Wopsle finished

4
00:00:14.619 --> 00:00:24.848
reading, the stranger said, "From information I've received, I believe there's a blacksmith here named Joe Gargery.

5
00:00:24.849 --> 00:00:27.015
Which one of you is that man?" "I am," said Joe. "You have an apprentice named Pip.

6
00:00:27.016 --> 00:00:30.515
Is he here?" "I'm here!" I cried. The stranger laid his hand on my shoulder. He did

7
00:00:30.516 --> 00:00:32.689
not recognize me, but I knew him as the gentleman I had met on the stairs the second

8
00:00:32.690 --> 00:00:35.181
time I visited Miss Havisham. His hands still smelled of scented soap. "I wish to have a

9
00:00:35.182 --> 00:00:44.745
private conversation with you two," said the man. "It will take some time. Perhaps we had better

10
00:00:44.746 --> 00:00:48.499
go to your place of residence." We left the others to wonder about us in silence and

11
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:53.428
walked home. Joe went ahead to open the front door and then led us into the parlor, which

12
00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:57.332
was feebly lighted by one candle. "My name is Jaggers," said the man when we had sat

13
00:00:57.333 --> 00:01:02.999
down. "I'm a lawyer in London and I'm pretty well known. I have some unusual business with

14
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.816
you. I come here not for myself but on behalf of another person. If this person had

15
00:01:06.817 --> 00:01:12.658
asked for my advice in the matter, I wouldn't be here. But my advice was not asked." Mr.

16
00:01:12.659 --> 00:01:22.898
Jaggers stood up. "Now, Joe Gargery, I am the bearer of an offer to relieve you of

17
00:01:22.899 --> 00:01:29.731
this apprentice. Would you object to canceling his indentures for his own good? And would you want

18
00:01:29.732 --> 00:01:36.382
anything for doing so?" "I would never stand in Pip's way," said Joe. "I'd want nothing." "I'm

19
00:01:36.383 --> 00:01:41.477
instructed to communicate to him," said Mr. Jaggers, pointing at me, "that he will one day receive a

20
00:01:41.478 --> 00:01:48.620
large sum of money. Furthermore it is the desire of his benefactor that he be immediately removed

21
00:01:48.621 --> 00:01:59.366
from this place and brought up as a gentleman—in other words, as a young fellow of great

22
00:01:59.367 --> 00:02:04.358
expectations." Joe and I gasped and looked at one another. My dream was coming true! Miss Havisham

23
00:02:04.359 --> 00:02:07.953
was going to make my fortune on a grand scale. "Now, Mr. Pip," said the lawyer. "You must

24
00:02:07.954 --> 00:02:17.850
first understand that it is your benefactor's wish that you always bear the name Pip. Do you

25
00:02:17.851 --> 00:02:22.755
have any objections to that?" My heart was beating so fast that I could barely say, "I

26
00:02:22.756 --> 00:02:30.263
have no objection." "I should think not!" said Mr. Jaggers. "Second you must understand that the name

27
00:02:30.264 --> 00:02:36.930
of your benefactor must remain a secret. I am allowed to mention that one day this person will

28
00:02:36.931 --> 00:02:43.565
reveal the name directly to you. But it may be years from now. You are prohibited from

29
00:02:43.566 --> 00:02:47.318
trying to discover the name. If you suspect who it is, keep that suspicion to yourself. If

30
00:02:47.319 --> 00:02:51.223
you have any objections to this, now is the time to speak." Once more I could barely

31
00:02:51.224 --> 00:02:59.231
get out the words to say that I had no objection. "You will please consider me your guardian.

32
00:02:59.232 --> 00:03:03.056
I have already been given a large sum of money sufficient for your living expenses and education.

33
00:03:03.057 --> 00:03:06.715
You must be educated in line with your new position in the world." I told Mr. Jaggers

34
00:03:06.716 --> 00:03:11.810
that I had always longed for more education. He said that he had a tutor in mind

35
00:03:11.811 --> 00:03:16.056
for me—a gentleman named Matthew Pocket. I'd heard that name at Miss Havisham's and understood him to be

36
00:03:16.057 --> 00:03:23.889
a relative of hers. "You should go to his house," said Mr. Jaggers, "but first you must

37
00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:29.953
meet his son who lives in London. When will you go to London?" I looked at Joe,

38
00:03:29.954 --> 00:03:37.961
who stood there motionless and gave me no help. "I suppose I could come right away." "First,"

39
00:03:37.962 --> 00:03:43.532
said Mr. Jaggers, "you should have some new clothes, and they should be a gentleman's clothes. Here is

40
00:03:43.533 --> 00:03:46.374
some money for you." He handed me twenty pounds. "Well, Joe Gargery, you look astonished." "I am

41
00:03:46.375 --> 00:03:49.342
astonished!" said Joe. "It was understood that you wanted nothing for yourself." "That's right." "But what if

42
00:03:49.343 --> 00:03:50.850
it were in my instructions to compensate you for the loss of Pip's services?" Joe laid his

43
00:03:50.851 --> 00:03:56.096
hand softly on my shoulder. Since then I have often thought of his unusual combination of strength and

44
00:03:56.097 --> 00:04:03.429
gentleness. "Pip is welcome to go to a life of honor and fortune. But if you think

45
00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:13.407
money can compensate me for the loss of the little child who came to the forge .

46
00:04:13.408 --> 00:04:18.764
. . who was ever the best of friends . . ." Oh, dear, good Joe, whom

47
00:04:18.765 --> 00:04:25.502
I was so ready to leave and to whom I was so ungrateful! I can still picture him

48
00:04:25.503 --> 00:04:31.915
with his muscular arm covering his eyes, and his broad chest heaving, and his voice dying away.

49
00:04:31.916 --> 00:04:35.169
He was crying! I struggled to comfort him and tell him that we would always be the

50
00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:39.907
best of friends. Mr. Jaggers tried one more time to offer Joe money. "I meant what I

51
00:04:39.908 --> 00:04:45.393
said." Joe raised his fists, ready to fight. "Come, Joe, it's all right." I pulled him away and

52
00:04:45.394 --> 00:04:53.218
he immediately calmed down. "Well, Mr. Pip, I think the sooner you leave here, the better," said

53
00:04:53.219 --> 00:04:57.544
Mr. Jaggers. "Come to me in London one week from today." I think he would have kept

54
00:04:57.545 --> 00:05:03.639
talking, except that he seemed to feel Joe was dangerous. He headed back to the pub, where

55
00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:12.615
he had left a hired carriage. Something came into my head to run after him. "I beg your

56
00:05:12.616 --> 00:05:15.187
pardon, sir. I want to follow your instructions, so I thought I'd better ask. Would anyone object

57
00:05:15.188 --> 00:05:17.711
if I went to see someone before I left?" "No. There is no problem with that." "Not

58
00:05:17.712 --> 00:05:19.274
even if this person lives uptown?" "That's fine." I ran home again and found my sister, Biddy,

59
00:05:19.275 --> 00:05:24.116
and Joe all sitting before the fire. After a while I said, "Joe, have you told Biddy?" "Pip's

60
00:05:24.117 --> 00:05:28.179
a gentleman of fortune now," said Joe, "and he's going to be leaving us for London." Biddy

61
00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:33.187
dropped her work and stared at me. After a pause she and Joe both congratulated me. We

62
00:05:33.188 --> 00:05:39.107
talked about my future, and they congratulated me again and expressed so much wonder at the idea

63
00:05:39.108 --> 00:05:46.186
of my being a gentleman that I didn't like it. It was soon time for bed. My room

64
00:05:46.187 --> 00:05:51.234
was hot, so I threw open my window. As I stood looking out, I saw Joe come

65
00:05:51.235 --> 00:05:57.480
out of the door below. Biddy brought his pipe and lit it for him. He never smoked

66
00:05:57.481 --> 00:06:04.337
so late, and it seemed to me that he wanted comforting. He and Biddy stood there talking

67
00:06:04.338 --> 00:06:08.917
quietly. More than once I heard my name mentioned in an endearing tone by both of them. I

68
00:06:08.918 --> 00:06:12.330
stepped away from the window and sat down in my one chair. It was very strange and

69
00:06:12.331 --> 00:06:19.314
sad, but this first night of my bright future was the loneliest one I had ever known.

